<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-provenance">
  <title>Provenance and quality</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"Real evidence is usually vague and unsatisfactory. It has to be examined - sifted."</p>
      <p>--- Agatha Christie, <em>The Mysterious Affair at Styles</em></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Graphics discover and communicate information, but the quality of that information depends on the quality and
      provenance of the data. Elegant visual design cannot compensate for weak source data.
    </p>
    <p>
      This chapter emphasizes checking origin, definitions, collection process, transformations, and downstream use so
      that analyses are credible and reproducible.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-prov-meaning">
    <title>What does provenance mean?</title>

    <p>
      Provenance is the documented origin and history of data: where they came from, who handled them, how they were
      collected, cleaned, transformed, and used. It is central to reproducibility and interpretation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Datasets are not born analysis-ready. Editing, coding changes, weighting, exclusions, and derived variables all
      affect conclusions and need explicit reporting.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-prov-problems">
    <title>What problems arise with datasets?</title>

    <p>
      Common issues include unclear definitions, awkward structure, missing or duplicated records, errors,
      inconsistencies, and hidden uncertainty. Graphics often help detect these issues, even when they cannot fully
      resolve them.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-definitions">
      <title>Data definitions</title>
      <p>
        Variable meaning is not always obvious. Definitions differ across countries, institutions, and time, and those
        differences can invalidate naive comparisons.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-collection">
      <title>Data collection</title>
      <p>
        Even clear definitions do not guarantee accurate measurement. Self-reported values, process drift, collection
        incentives, and question wording can all bias data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-availability">
      <title>Data availability</title>
      <p>
        Publicly available data often require major wrangling and restructuring. Keeping raw originals and retaining
        all cleaning code are essential for auditability and reruns.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-prov-book-datasets">
    <title>The provenance of datasets used in the book</title>

    <p>
      The examples below illustrate why provenance checks matter in practice and how deeper source work can change
      analysis quality.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-gapminder">
      <title>Gapminder</title>
      <p>
        Gapminder provides extensive source and cleaning notes, but country-year quality is uneven and must still be
        treated cautiously in comparisons.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-movies">
      <title>Movies</title>
      <p>
        IMDb-derived data are dynamic and heavily filtered before analysis. Genre coding, runtime variants, and user-
        profile uncertainty all affect interpretation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-1912">
      <title>Democratic Convention 1912</title>
      <p>
        Adding session-time context (beyond ballot order alone) revealed timing structure and likely influence of
        informal between-session negotiations.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-lightspeed">
      <title>Speed of light</title>
      <p>
        Classical speed-of-light datasets are often simplified in teaching. Original experiment reports show procedural
        changes that challenge assumptions such as i.i.d. sampling.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-olympics">
      <title>Olympic Games</title>
      <p>
        Long historical spans introduce changing countries, event naming inconsistencies, and missing values that
        require explicit harmonization decisions.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-olyamdx-prov">
        <image source="images/olyAmdx-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Athletics field events for men: percentage differences in gold-medal performances.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-chess">
      <title>Chess ratings</title>
      <p>
        Multi-year federation files reveal format changes, coding changes, stale records, and category ambiguities
        that demand careful cleaning and reconciliation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-gayrights">
      <title>Attitudes to same-sex marriage</title>
      <p>
        Question wording, answer coding, and subsampling design all influence conclusions; obtaining raw survey details
        can materially change interpretation.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-gayfss">
        <image source="images/gayFSs-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Responses on same-sex marriage at state level versus federal constitutional ban.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-astronauts">
      <title>Human spaceflights</title>
      <p>
        Scraped datasets can contain inconsistencies and errors from unknown points in the pipeline; interesting data
        still require rigorous checking.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-diamonds">
      <title>Diamonds</title>
      <p>
        Convenience sampling, impossible values, gaps, and heaping all appear. These issues do not make data useless,
        but they do constrain inference.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-histcarx">
        <image source="images/histCarX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Histogram of diamond carats (showing heaping).</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-electric">
      <title>Electric car charging</title>
      <p>
        Timeline structure and staged infrastructure rollout are crucial context; early periods may be unsuitable as
        stable baselines for behavior.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-elecstat2x">
        <image source="images/elecStat2x-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Use of charging stations grouped by location, with test-period endpoint marked.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-finches">
      <title>Darwin's finches</title>
      <p>
        Historical expeditions may not match modern standards, but can still provide strong species-separation signals
        when interpreted with proper caveats.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-snodpcpx">
        <image source="images/snodPCPx-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Parallel coordinate plot of Galapagos finch measurements.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-vehicles">
      <title>Vehicle fuel consumption</title>
      <p>
        Class definitions may differ from intuition. Clarifying formal definitions with data providers can explain
        apparently anomalous category assignments.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-comrades">
      <title>Comrades Marathon</title>
      <p>
        Contextual race-profile information changes interpretation of "up" versus "down" runs and helps explain
        persistent performance differences.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-comprofx">
        <image source="images/comProfX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Profile plot for the planned 2020 down race.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-shearwaters">
      <title>Shearwaters</title>
      <p>
        The same dataset appears under different labels and modified codings across packages, creating confusion and
        highlighting the need for source verification.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-titanic">
      <title>Titanic</title>
      <p>
        Multiple Titanic datasets differ in detail and quality. Modern curated sources provide richer crew and age
        detail that materially changes subgroup survival analysis.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-titandd">
        <image source="images/titanDD-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Titanic survival by class/crew: older summary dataset versus newer detailed dataset.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-prov-invented-simulated-real">
    <title>How do invented, simulated, and real datasets compare?</title>

    <p>
      Invented and simulated datasets are useful in specific contexts, but real datasets better expose practical
      issues, support contextual checking, and train defensible analysis habits.
    </p>

    <p>
      Real does not mean unquestionable. Datasets can be over-trusted or unfairly dismissed; both mistakes are
      avoidable with careful provenance work.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-prov-main-points">
      <title>Main points</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Dataset provenance - source, edits, and processing history - is essential.</p></li>
        <li><p>Large real datasets are increasingly available but require extensive checking.</p></li>
        <li><p>Each dataset has unique issues; no single checklist solves everything.</p></li>
        <li><p>Real datasets are generally preferable to invented or purely simulated examples for applied work.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
